
This example illustrates the type of calculation described in
http://dx.doi.org/10.1063/1.4928372 and the related paper by
F. Scotti et al., Nucl. Fusion (2020). The efit2dg2d tool
was developed for this application as a replacement for an
analogous IDL-based routine.

The focus on the emitted Balmer-alpha light in the relatively
low temperature edge region of the plasma also makes this
example ideal for illustrating the effect of the relatively
new H2 collisional radiative data set.

1. Copy the degas2.in file to your run directory.

2. This run uses only standard DEGAS 2 reference data, so 
one should not need to run datasetup before beginning this
simulation.

3. Run efit2dg2d:
   - Note that efit2dg2d is still relatively new and does not
     yet possess an input file.
   - One is instead limited to three existing applications controlled
     by the APP macro near the top of efit2dg2d.web.
   - For this example, edit this line to read:

@m APP NSTX_ENDD

   - Then, in the run directory:

gmake efit2dg2d
efit2dg2d ../examples/NSTX_204500_682_ex/g204500.00682

    - This produces the following files:
      * wallfile - set of flux surface contours that will be read by
                   definegeometry2d.
      * polygons_dg2d.in - set of polygons constructed out of these
                           contours; this will form the bulk of the
                           input file to definegeometry2d.
      * stratum_psi - Mapping between the stratum values used to label
                      the polygons in polygons_dg2d.in and flux surface
                      labels, such as major radius.  This will be read
                      by defineback.
       * polygons.txt - Explicit coordinate listing of the polygons
                        for plotting by the user; i.e., this is not
                        otherwise needed to run this example.

     - These files are also provided as part of this example, just
       ensure that you are able to reproduce them.

4. Run definegeometry2d:
   - The input file nstx_204500_682_dg2d.in has been constructed from
     the polygons_dg2d.in file generated efit2dg2d by adding sections
     at the top and bottom:
   - The lines at the top specify the symmetry, the extent of the
     universal cell (bounds), the location of the wallfile (also 
     from efit2dg2d), and the "end_prep" command indicating the
     end of the input's preamble section.
   - At the bottom:
     * Two polygons surrounding the contour "box" defined by the
       polygons in polygons_dg2d.in.  These are constructed using
       the "outer" keyword and points from the "boundary wall",
       wall number 62 (the last wall) in the wallfile.  The logic
       used in selecting the points from this wall is sufficiently
       straightforward that we leave its explanation as an exercise
       for the user.
    
     * The location of the polygon_nc_file.  This file will be
       used by defineback.

     * The "end" keyword indicating the end of the input file.

definegeometry2d ../examples/NSTX_204500_682_ex/nstx_204500_682_dg2d.in

5. Run problemsetup

6. Run defineback
   - Copy the nstxendd3.web file to the src directory as usr2dplasma.web (see
     instructions at the top of the file) and compile defineback.
   - The input file for defineback is nstx_204500_682_db.in,
     * Note that the arguments for the plasma_file keyword are two files
       generated by the above steps, stratum_psi and poly_204500_682.nc;
       these are read from your run directory.
     * The file argument in between them provides the plasma parameters as a
       function of a radial coordinate.
     * The final two floating point numbers are e-folding lengths used for
       extrapolating the profiles beyond those data.  The large values 
       shown here effectively amount to constant extrapolation.

defineback ../examples/NSTX_204500_682_ex/nstx_204500_682_db.in

7. Run tallysetup

8. Set the desired number of flights in the background netCDF file and run
   flighttest.
   - This example uses 100,000 flights.

9. Post-processing
   - Start by compiling and running ucd_plot:

ucd_plot poly_204500_682.nc

      * This yields a 2-D plot of the entire system.

    - However, the principal output of the ENDD diagnostic is a
      1-D, radial profile of the neutral density at midplane.
      * To generate a comparable profile, set the macro flag
        in plotgeom.web:

@m MIDSLICE 1

       * Then compile geomtesta.
       * This input file takes a narrow slice through the
         the geometry at midplane.
       * The macro hack to plotgeom.web averages this over
         the vertical pixels and writes the output to
	 text files as a function of major radius.
       * These include the D and D2 densities, as well as the
         Balmer alpha emissivities by species and total.

10. Illustrate H2 CR model effects
    - Copy degas2_jcr.in to the work directory as degas2.in
    - Rerun problemsetup, tallysetup, flighttest, and the post-processing
      routines as above.
      * Note that the same geometry and background netCDF files can be
        used.
      * The problem, tally, and output netCDF files have different names
        to distinguish them from the ones used above.
      * The resulting output files from geomtesta have a "CR" in their
        names.
	
	 
    







